<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry IV Probus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1258–<a href="/years/1290">1290</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Henry IV the Righteous</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry IV Probus was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a> from 1270 and High Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> from 1288 to 1290. Raised at the Bohemian court, he was a patron of German poetry and wrote verse in Middle High German himself. He sought a royal crown for Poland, but died young, possibly poisoned, and his ambitions passed with his lands to rival princes.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-iv-probus.jpg" alt="Henry IV Probus" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Qkiel · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Henryk_Probus,_orygina%C5%82,_Wroc%C5%82aw_1a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Henry Probus was the only son of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-the-white">Henry III the White</a> of Silesia-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a> and Judith of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masovia">Masovia</a>. His father died in 1266, leaving him a minor, and his uncle Archbishop Władysław of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> became his guardian. In 1267 Władysław sent him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> to be raised at the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia">Ottokar II of Bohemia</a>. After Władysław died in 1270 Henry returned to Wrocław and received a careful education, which may explain his later interest in culture and poetry. He was formally proclaimed an adult in 1273 and began a policy more independent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1277 Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-ii-the-bald">Bolesław II the Bald</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legnica">Legnica</a> seized him at Jelcz and imprisoned him. He was freed at the end of the year after giving up a third of his duchy and pledging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krosno-odrzanskie">Krosno Odrzańskie</a> for his ransom. Between 1282 and 1287 he fought Bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-ii-zaremba">Thomas II Zaremba</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a> over land and jurisdiction; he was excommunicated in March 1284, but the bishop submitted in 1287. In 1280 he went to King Rudolph I in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, where he tried to obtain the Polish royal crown. He granted towns privileges under Magdeburg Law and encouraged the settlement of artisans and merchants, and two poems are recorded under his name in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-manesse">Codex Manesse</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leszek-the-black">Leszek II the Black</a> died on 30 September 1288, Henry contested <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> against Władysław I the Elbow-high. His allies were defeated at the Battle of Siewierz on 26 February 1289. In August 1289, helped by the townspeople and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a>, he took Kraków and was recognised as High Duke. He died suddenly in 1290, aged no more than thirty-two.</p><p>Ottokar of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> told that he was poisoned after a quarrel with the pope over money sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, though only some elements are confirmed by other sources. His will left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-glogow">Henry III of Głogów</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl-ii">Przemysł II</a>. In the event <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-v-the-fat">Henry V the Fat</a> took Wrocław, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wenceslaus-ii-of-bohemia">Wenceslaus II of Bohemia</a> gained the Kraków lands, while Przemysł II kept the title of High Duke.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry Probus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the Silesian <b>Henry IV Probus</b> and the Bohemian king Wenceslaus II, who ruled from 1291, Wladyslaw I the Elbow-high took Krakow in 1306.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-krakow">Duchy of Krakow</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1290 Duke <b>Henry IV Probus</b> granted Bishop Thomas II full princely rights there, making the territory an episcopal duchy.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-nysa">Duchy of Nysa</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Henry IV Probus</b>, also remembered as a minnesinger, won Kraków and tried to unite Poland before his death in 1290.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-wroclaw">Duchy of Wrocław</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.11,17.03&amp;year=1278" aria-label="Wrocław on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Henry IV Probus"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4310 5689h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4310 5689h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.11,17.03&amp;year=1278">What was here in 1278? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
